  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1242 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday begins the long term period with the by then ~565dm H5 low \ntracking westward into northwestern NM bringing a cold front west to \neast across the state thru the day. Showers will continue to favor \nthe northern tier of the state with any snow relegated to the higher \nelevations of the northern mountains. Mostly dry and gusty \nconditions will continue to favor a SW-NE corridor across the state \nthru Wednesday with winds veering westerly and northwesterly behind \nthe attendant surface cold front. Numerical model guidance is also \nin fair agreement that some percentage of moisture from TC Simon \nalong the western Mexican coastline will get picked up and advected \nnorthward. Upper level forcing will be able to generate some \nthunderstorms over the southeastern tier of the state. Ample shear \nwill allow some these storms to threaten severe gusts, and there is \nalready a marginal risk for severe weather set out by the SPC. \n\nA cool and dry fall airmass fills in west to east behind the surface \ncold front Wednesday night into Thursday morning. With calming wind \nspeeds overnight, radiational cooling will allow for low \ntemperatures to plummet at many locations. Northern valley locations \nin particular will likely see their first freezes over the season, \naside from the Moreno Valley where lows have already been regularly \nbelow freezing at times. \n\nThereafter, numerical and AI model suites have come into much better \nagreement with a piece of upper level energy from the polar jet \nbeing squeezed off over the Desert Southwest, generating its own new \nlarge but weak upper low Thursday. Subsidence behind the departing \nmain upper low will send down a potent cold front, backing south and \nwest through eastern NM. This front is progged to continue westward, \nsurging thru the gaps and over the ridgelines of the central \nmountain chain late Thursday through Thursday night. The GFS and \nNAM12 in particular have strong H7 easterly winds of 40-50kts during \nthis time, hence the thinking this will overtop the Sandia/Manzano's \nproducing gusty easterly winds all areas of the ABQ metro outside of \njust the Tijeras Canyon. Wind Advisories and potentially even High \nWind Warnings look to be hoisted for the Thursday night through \nFriday morning period for this event, focusing on those strong \neasterly winds gusting to 45-55mph, potentially higher, through the \ncentral mountain chain. \n\nSome forecast uncertainty remains regarding how much moisture from \nthe remnants of TC Simon gets pulled northward by this new upper low \nand into the Big Bend and southern NM area Friday and into next \nweekend. Regardless of the degree, precipitation chances will favor \nthe southern third or so of NM Friday and heading into next\nweekend.",
